From d197d823f329af6e29545858897b30aaf366cb91 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: luoxiaoyu <137460387@qq.com>
Date: Wed, 11 Nov 2026 10:19:32 +0800
Subject: [PATCH] Writing a stick: re-identify the disk inside the erase, and
 never reuse a stale number

The read-only check ran in a separate PowerShell process, then the raw erase opened
PhysicalDriveN and deleted its partition table without asking what was behind that number.
Windows can renumber a USB disk between the two, so the number could belong to another disk.
The format fallback also returned the picked number when no unique serial/size/model match
was found, and the full-installer partition used the pre-erase number.

- rawdisk.wipe_mbr_fat32(..., expected=disk) queries the open handle (serial, vendor/product,
  size) and refuses before IOCTL_DISK_DELETE_DRIVE_LAYOUT unless it is the picked stick.
- current_number() returns None instead of guessing; both diskpart fallbacks stop and say so.
- The format script no longer falls back to Get-Disk -Number; it reports the disk it found, and
  the second partition uses that final number.
- WMI erase path verifies the physical disk identity before diskpart.
- Regression tests: tests/test_usb_erase_identity.py, updated renumbering tests.
